          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is coronary artery disease and how does it develop over time?",
          "answer": "Coronary artery disease is the build-up of fatty plaque in the arteries that supply the heart muscle. The reference summary says plaque forms over decades. It starts with injury to the inner lining of the artery, followed by a build-up of cholesterol-rich material beneath that lining (*Coronary artery disease*, General clinical reference).\n\nThe same summary describes two different ways the disease can cause symptoms:\n\n- **Gradual narrowing.** As plaque grows, the artery opens less. Blood flow can then fall short when the heart needs more, usually during exertion. This causes predictable chest discomfort called stable angina.\n- **Plaque rupture.** A plaque can crack open. This exposes material that can trigger a blood clot, which may block the artery suddenly. This is an acute coronary syndrome, and it can happen at a plaque that was never narrow enough to cause symptoms. A past normal stress test does not rule out a future heart attack.\n\nThe passages I retrieved do not describe the stages in detail between early plaque and symptoms, or how fast this varies between people, so I can't give a fuller timeline from them. A clinician can discuss personal risk and monitoring.\n\n**Seek emergency care now** if you have chest pressure, tightness or squeezing that lasts more than a few minutes or keeps coming back, especially with pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, breathlessness, sweating, nausea or light-headedness. These can be signs of a heart attack (*Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*, emergency guidance).\n\nFor coding, coronary artery disease falls in ICD-10-CM codes I20 to I25 within the circulatory system chapter (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 9*).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
